entries.english:
Johann Gottfried [von Guttenberg], Bischof zu Würzburg, grants the brothers and sons of Franz Rudolf von Rosenbach—Philipp Ludwig, Domherr zu Bamberg und Würzburg; Franz Albrecht; and Anton Philibert von Rosenbach—for themselves, and then to Philipp Franz as representative of his brother Johann Hartmann, who is away travelling, the following properties as male fiefs:
- Thundorf Castle with all rights, appurtenances, and revenues, except only the farmstead;
- the deserted settlement of Ransbach, with its mill and farmstead and all rights and revenues;
- the villages of Theinfeld and Rothhausen, with all rights, holdings, and revenues there;
- an estate in Maßbach behind the churchyard;
- the estates in Poppenlauer;
- the estates in Ebertshausen;
- half of the tithe at Altenmünster;
- one third of the tithe at Oberlauringen;
- one eighth of the tithe at Alsleben;
- one quarter of the tithe at Ober- and Untereßfeld;
- a vineyard and two woods at Thundorf.
In addition, the bishop grants them the following properties, which had belonged outright to Veit Ulrich von Schaumberg and which he then enfeoffed to the Würzburg bishopric:
- the farmstead and vineyards at Thundorf;
- the lake at Weißensee;
- a fishing right in the Lauer below Maßbach;
- a fishing right in the Lauer within the Rothhausen district;
- three quarters of the mill at Rothhausen;
- two estates at Theinfeld;
- two freehold estates at Weichtungen;
- four rent-paying fiefs at Stadtlauringen;
- nine estates and eleven fiefs in Maßbach;
- the Brandmühle below Maßbach;
- five estates and four fiefs in Poppenlauer;
- three estates and one fief in Nüdlingen;
- one fief in Rannungen;
- a field estate in Hesselbach;
- the Herlingsberg wood;
- the male fief at Prappach.
They purchased these properties from the brothers Georg Dietrich, Wilhelm Ludwig, Wilhelm Ulrich, Georg Wilhelm, Johann Sigmund, Johann Philipp, and Johann Wilhelm von Schaumberg.
Finally, the bishop also grants them the following properties, originally held as fiefs from the County of Henneberg and then from the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar, which passed to Würzburg as a result of an agreement between the Würzburg bishopric and the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar:
- the district court, now moved to Poppenlauer, with all appurtenances and rights, excluding the villages of Maßbach and Volkershausen;
- half of the bailiwick in Poppenlauer;
- two estates in Maßbach and a meadow between Maßbach and Rothhausen;
- one quarter of the tithe at Zeil am Main;
- one third of one quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main;
- four woods near Maßbach;
- the wine rents and rent revenues at Schonungen. Should the von Rosenbach family ever have no male heirs, the Würzburg bishopric is obliged to pay the family’s female heirs 2,000 Reichstaler in return for these revenues;
- one third and one quarter of a whole quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main.
Sealer: the bishop, with his privy seal.

Notes: Abstract (18th century).
